>> title: Wedge Salad
>> category: Salads
>> tags: iceberg, bacon, blue cheese, steakhouse, no-cook
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: wedge salad, iceberg wedge, blue cheese wedge, steakhouse wedge, lettuce wedge, quarter wedge
>> pairs-with: blue-cheese-dressing, ranch-dressing
>> servings: 4
>> time: 35 min
>> slack: narrow — the wedge has to be cold enough to sweat, and a head left out on the bench never gets there again inside the hour
>> step.1: render 10 min
>> step.2: pickle 20 min
>> step.3: quarter through the core
>> step.4: spoon over

Render @thick-cut bacon{4%slices}(cut in 1/2-in lardons) in a cold #skillet{} over medium heat for ~render{10%min}, until the fat is gone out of them and the pieces rattle. Lardons rather than crumbles: a wedge is eaten with a knife, and something you can actually spear belongs on it.

Pickle @shallot{1%large}(sliced in thin rings) in @red wine vinegar{1/4%cup}(60 mL) with @sugar{1%tsp} and @kosher salt{1/2%tsp} for ~stand{20%min}. This is the piece the steakhouse version leaves out and the piece the salad needs — everything else on the plate is fat on fat, and nothing cuts it.

Quarter @iceberg lettuce{1%head}(cored, chilled hard for an hour) straight down through the stem so each quarter holds together. Iceberg is here for cold and crunch and nothing else, and both of those are a refrigerator job — a room-temperature wedge is a limp wedge no dressing can rescue.

Spoon @blue cheese dressing{3/4%cup}(180 mL; cold, from the drawer) over @&(~1)wedges{} and scatter @&(~3)rendered lardons{}, @&(~2)pickled shallots{}, @blue cheese{2%oz}(60 g; crumbled), @chives{2%Tbs}(sliced thin) and @black pepper{1/2%tsp} across them.

Poured, never tossed, and dressed at the last minute in front of whoever is eating it. A wedge that has sat dressed is a bowl of cold soup with a lettuce in it.
